 digits would warrant a closer look.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "South Dakota sits roughly fifteen points to the right of the nation in presidential voting, and Republicans have carried the state by double digits in every recent cycle. No state-specific polls or expert ratings exist for this open contest, in which the term-limited incumbent leaves both parties with new standard-bearers. Expectations therefore rest on the state's durable partisan lean.\n\nA strong national environment could trim the Republican margin, as it would anywhere, but South Dakota has not been seriously contested at the presidential level in decades. The state's first-ever gubernatorial runoff this year shows active politics, yet nothing suggests the presidential race will deviate from form. A comfortable Republican win is the expectation, with the main uncertainty coming from the absence of local polling rather than any evidence of competitiveness.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0482, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0894, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0882, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0848, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0844, "margin": -15.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0865, "margin": -15.0}]}